.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt PING 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ping
.Nd send ICMP and ICMPv6 echo requests
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nq46
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl t Ar ttl
.Op Fl W Ar timeout
.Op Fl w Ar deadline
.Ar host
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends ICMP echo requests to
.Ar host ,
one a second, and prints each reply's line, with its size, its sequence
number, the hop limit it arrived with as
.Ql ttl=
and its round-trip time, until
.Ar count
requests are sent, the deadline passes, or an interrupt comes; it then
prints the statistics, in the form Linux's iputils
.Nm
prints them.
Root privilege is not required.
.Pp
The host may be an IPv4 address, a name, or an IPv6 address.
A link-local IPv6 address, or a group of link scope, may be followed by
.Ql %
and the name or index of the interface it is reached by, as in
.Ql fe80::1%eth0 ,
which a machine with more than one card requires.
Names resolve only to IPv4 addresses.
Names are looked up in
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
or
.Pa /hosts
when that file is absent, then through a DNS A query to the first valid
.Ql nameserver
in
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
or
.Pa /resolv.conf
when that file is absent.
The default DNS server is
.Ql 10.0.2.3 .
The resolver retries for about six seconds unless a matching response
arrives.
.Pp
Each request uses the kernel's private socket operation 0x1001,
.Dv SC_PING_DATA ,
described in
.Xr ping 2 ,
which takes the destination, the data size and the hop limit, or for an
IPv6 address operation 0x1002,
.Dv SC_PING6 ,
which takes the interface as well.
musl has no wrapper for it, so
.Nm
issues it through a direct
.Xr socketcall 2
on i686, or Rach2's call 400 on x86-64, which has no socketcall.
The kernel sends an echo of 56 bytes of data, 84 with its headers, as
iputils sends by default, or the size of
.Fl s ,
with the hop limit 64 or that of
.Fl t ,
and waits for the reply for up to four attempts of a second each; a signal
ends the wait.
.Nm
times the round trip on the monotonic clock, to the microsecond, and a
request with no reply in that time is counted as lost and not printed.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-w deadline"
.It Fl c Ar count
Stop after
.Ar count
requests.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Send a request every
.Ar interval
seconds, which may have a fraction, instead of every second.
.It Fl s Ar size
Send
.Ar size
bytes of data in each request, from 0 to 65507, or 65527 for IPv6.
A request past one datagram of the 1500-byte MTU leaves in fragments,
and its reply is reassembled.
.It Fl t Ar ttl
Send each request with the hop limit
.Ar ttl ,
from 1 to 255, instead of 64.
.It Fl W Ar timeout
Count a request as lost when its reply has not come in
.Ar timeout
seconds.
.It Fl w Ar deadline
Stop when
.Ar deadline
seconds have passed since the first request.
.It Fl q
Print only the first line and the statistics.
.It Fl 6
Require an IPv6 address.
.It Fl n , Fl 4
Accepted and ignored: replies are printed by address, and a request is
IPv4 unless its address is IPv6.
.El
.Pp
For IPv6 the first line has the form iputils gives it,
.Ql PING ::1(::1) 56 data bytes .
The kernel resolves no IPv6 neighbour yet, so only ::1, this machine's
own addresses and groups answer; a unicast address on another host
fails with
.Ql ping: sendmsg: Host is unreachable .
.Pp
The kernel permits only one request in flight for the whole machine.
If another request is in flight, the operation fails with
.Er EBUSY
and
.Nm
stops with a diagnostic rather than reporting a lost request.
.Pp
On os-real, this program is installed as
.Pa /bin/ping .
BusyBox's ping applet is not built.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/resolv.conf
.It Pa /etc/hosts
Host names and addresses.
.It Pa /etc/resolv.conf
DNS server configuration.
.It Pa /hosts , /resolv.conf
The files read when the two above are absent.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 when a reply arrived, and with both
.Fl c
and
.Fl w ,
when
.Ar count
replies arrived.
It returns 1 when they did not, and 2 for an option it does not take, a
count or a time that is not a number, a host it cannot resolve, a
request the kernel cannot send, and another request in flight.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl ping -c 3 192.168.0.1
.Dl ping -c 2 -W 1 github.com
.Dl ping -q -w 5 1.1.1.1
.Dl ping -6 -c 2 ::1
.Dl ping -c 2 ff02::1%eth0
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql ping: another ping is in flight on this machine
Another ICMP echo request is in progress.
.It Ql ping: HOST: cannot resolve the name
The host is neither an address nor a name the files or DNS answer for.
.It Ql ping: HOST: not an IPv6 address
.Fl 6
was given with a host that is not an IPv6 address.
.It Ql ping: NAME: no such interface
The interface after
.Ql %
is neither an index nor the name of an interface.
.It Ql ping: sendmsg: MESSAGE
The kernel could not send the first IPv6 request, for the reason
.Xr strerror 3
gives.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr ping 2
